---
import Card from "./Card/index.astro";
import type {Goal} from "../lib/constants";
import {goalStatusLine, nextRace, nextRaceLine, patchesEarned} from "../lib/projection";
import EventsLink from './EventsLink.astro';
import ProgressBar from './ProgressBar.astro';

interface Props {
    goal: Goal;
}

// THE CARD IS ONE NUMBER, A RULE UNDER IT, TWO SENTENCES AND A WAY OUT — in that order,
// and the order is the argument. A visitor came for the figure, so the figure is the
// first and loudest thing; the rule shows the same fact as a shape; the two lines say
// what the figure implies about the rest of the year; the control says where to see the
// races themselves. Every element does exactly one job, which is what the body did not
// do before: a 24px pill carried the fraction AND the sport's icon while a line beneath
// restated the fraction in prose, and a bordered chip both reported the countdown and
// navigated. Deleting the pill's icon, its capsule and the restated line is what paid
// for the extra row, and the card came out SHORTER than the version it replaced.
//
// THE FIGURE IS ANNOUNCED BY THE MEASURE, NOT BY ITSELF. The hero is aria-hidden and
// the progress bar directly under it carries `aria-valuetext` with the same two numbers
// in words a screen reader can say — "2279.7 of 5000 km" rather than a slash. Hiding it
// removes a duplicate announcement rather than information, and the pairing is gated:
// tests/rendered-html.test.ts fails if the hero is taken out of the tree without an equivalent
// `aria-valuetext` beside it, so the meter cannot quietly become decorative later.
//
// THE BUDGET THIS NOTE GUARDED IS GONE, AND THE WHOLE OF WHAT FOLLOWS IS HISTORY.
//
// It was real while `main` had a height CAP and eight equal fraction rows: the
// right-hand stack got a set slice of a set grid, so a line added to any of its
// three cards came out of the other two. `main`'s row template sizes to its content now
// and the cap is gone (index.astro), so a fourth or fifth row makes the GRID taller and
// the page scroll instead.
//
// Measured, by building the same mutation against both revisions rather than by reasoning
// about either. One extra `text-xs` line into one goal card, at 1024x600:
//
//     before   Now 154 -> 150.7 -> 146.7px, the OTHER goal card 232.8 -> 227 -> 220px
//     after    Now 154px and the other goal card 232.8px, both unmoved; only the edited
//              card grows, 232.8 -> 252.8 -> 272.8px
//
// So the rule "anything added to either goal card or to Now must remove something first"
// no longer holds, and neither does the 4.4px. What is still true is that the page gets
// taller: at a 797px-tall viewport and above it does not scroll today, and enough new rows
// will change that. Weigh it against the page, not against a budget.
//
// The arithmetic below is kept because the CARD-LEVEL figures in it are still current and
// still the cheapest way to price a design change here — 232.8px per card, the Now card at
// 154px, the cost of a text-xs row at 20px. Read the "unspent" column as the state of a
// mechanism that no longer exists.
//
// The right-hand stack (index.astro) pooled its leftover height at its foot, and the
// tightest lg configuration was any viewport whose height put `main` on its 46rem floor
// while lg was still active — 1024x600, 1152x700 and 1280x720 all tied. Measured on the
// built page at those three:
//
//     the pill revision that shipped before this   234.0px per card,  2.0px unspent
//     this revision                                 232.8px per card,  4.4px unspent
//
// So the redesign is CHEAPER than what it replaced, which is the only reason a fourth row
// was affordable at all: the pill was 24px of card and the line restating its fraction was
// another 20, against 22.8 for the hero and 2 for the rule. At the roomy configurations the
// stack's leftover goes 66px to 68.4px. The Now card is untouched at 154px throughout —
// that figure is the one to watch, because it is what moves first when this runs out.
//
// At zero the flex column starts CONTRACTING its children and takes the Now card down
// with the goal cards (154 -> 149.39px) even though nothing was added to it. That, not
// clipping, is the cost the budget is protecting against: glyphs are not sheared until
// FOUR extra lines, which three independent rebuilds confirmed at 0 ink lost across 43,
// 170 and 170 lg configurations.
//
// BE CAREFUL QUOTING THAT HEADROOM HERE, because this paragraph is about the TIGHT
// configurations and the four-line figure was not measured at them: those sweeps ran at
// roomy viewports, where the stack has 68.4px rather than 4.4px. At the tight end the
// contraction starts at the FIRST extra line — that is what "4.4px unspent" means, since a
// text-xs row costs 20px in each of two cards. Four lines is the shearing threshold, not
// the budget; the budget above is the number that governs.
//
// `progress_last_year` is still configured and nothing renders it — deliberately, so it
// is one edit from returning, and the January checklist in constants.ts still asks for
// it. If it comes back, something else has to leave.
//
// Nothing in the suite can see any of this: there is no layout engine in the tests, so a
// taller variant runs green. The guards are the width bound in tests/projection.test.ts,
// the text-relative-sizing assertions in tests/rendered-html.test.ts, and the browser
// sweep in the PR — never a green run.
//
// (The verb above is deliberately not the obvious one — the obvious one is a flex
// utility, and UnoCSS scans this comment, so writing it emits a real CSS rule that
// no element wears. tests/build-output.test.ts catches that by name.)
const {goal} = Astro.props;
const statusLine = goalStatusLine(goal);
const countdown = nextRaceLine(nextRace(goal.sport), patchesEarned(goal.sport));
---

{/* SENTENCE CASE, because the control 250px below in this same card already writes it
     that way: `NEXT_RACE.control` lowercases the sport for "My cycling events", and the
     wall it opens is headed with those very words. The heading said "My Cycling goal"
     — a mid-sentence capital the card then contradicted in its own button. `goal_name`
     is a DISPLAY name and is capitalised for the places that need it; the casing
     belongs to the sentence, not to the field. */}
<Card colSpan="md:col-span-1" rowSpan="md:row-span-3" title=`My ${goal.goal_name.toLowerCase()} goal this year`>
    <p class="goal-figure" aria-hidden="true">{goal.current_progress}<span
            class="goal-figure-target"> / {goal.total_goal} {goal.measurable_unit}</span></p>
    <ProgressBar
            currentProgress={goal.current_progress}
            totalGoal={goal.total_goal}
            label=`${goal.goal_name} progress`
            unit={goal.measurable_unit}
    />
    <div class="goal-lines text-xs font-light">
        {statusLine && <span>{statusLine}</span>}
        <span>{countdown}</span>
        <EventsLink goal={goal}/>
    </div>
</Card>

<style>
    /*
     * THE ONE BOLD THING ON THE CARD. The digits are set on one advance width because the
     * two cards' figures sit one above the other in the same column and ragged digit
     * widths make them read as misaligned; the negative tracking and the sub-1 line
     * height are what let a 1.5rem number sit in a 182px row without looking like a
     * headline. (The declaration's own value is blocklisted in uno.config.ts — UnoCSS
     * reads the CSS below and would emit a utility rule for it that nothing wears.)
     *
     * 1.5rem IS A MEASURED CEILING, and the reason is HEIGHT rather than wrapping — which
     * is worth saying plainly, because the variant rig this design came out of said
     * otherwise and it was wrong. In the rig 1.75rem wrapped the cycling figure at 1024px
     * and desynchronised the pair to 227.5 and 251.7px. In the real component it does not
     * wrap at all: measured across 20 configurations (five lg widths x four root sizes),
     * the hero is one line at both sizes and the two cards stay identical. What 1.75rem
     * actually costs is 3.8px per card — 236.6 against 232.8 — which was more than the
     * 4.4px the right-hand stack then had in hand across two cards, so it would have put
     * the stack back into contraction. The ceiling is real; the story about it was not.
     *
     * That last reason has EXPIRED: the stack no longer has a set height to run out of
     * (see the note at the top of this file). 1.5rem stays, because it is the size the
     * design was chosen at and 7.6px of page height is still 7.6px, but it is now a taste
     * decision rather than an arithmetic one. Do not quote the contraction argument for it.
     *
     * So: if this copy grows, measure the STACK, not the line count. A wrap would show up
     * as the hero's own height exceeding one of its own line-heights — and note that
     * neither `getClientRects()` on this paragraph nor a Range over its contents answers
     * that question, the first because a paragraph has one border box whatever it
     * holds, the second because the denominator is half the size and so sits at its own
     * top even on a shared line. Both reported a wrap that was not there.
     */
    .goal-figure {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: -0.04em;
        line-height: 0.95;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }

    /*
     * Half the hero, which lands the target on the same 0.75rem as the two lines below —
     * so the card has exactly two text sizes and the denominator belongs to the smaller
     * one. It is a ratio rather than a second absolute size so that the pair scales
     * together; a pinned length here would break the relationship at any other root size.
     */
    .goal-figure-target {
        font-size: 0.5em;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }

    /*
     * Every length here is font-relative for the reason the whole page is: the card
     * clips, so a gap pinned in device pixels deletes text the moment the reader
     * enlarges theirs. The rule's own 2px stroke is the deliberate exception — see
     * ProgressBar.astro.
     *
     * THIS GAP IS THE TIGHTEST ON THE CARD, and both reasons it is 0.25rem are worth
     * keeping. The rule measures the number directly above it, so pulling it close is
     * what makes the pair read as one object rather than as a figure and a separate
     * graphic; and it is where the redesign's extra row was paid for. At 0.5rem the
     * card is 236.8px, which is 2.8px more than the pill revision it replaced; at 0.25rem
     * the card is 232.8px. Measured on the built page.
     *
     * The reason RECORDED here for preferring 0.25rem has expired — it was that 0.5rem
     * took the right-hand stack to zero unspent height and started the flex column
     * contracting the Now card. There is no set stack height to exhaust any more (see
     * the note at the top of this file), so what 0.5rem costs now is 4px of page, not a
     * neighbouring card. The value stays for the optical reason in the first sentence.
     */
    .goal-figure + :global([role="progressbar"]) {
        margin-top: 0.25rem;
    }

    .goal-lines {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 0.25rem;
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
    }

    /*
     * The control gets more air above it than the lines give each other, because it is
     * not one of them: the two sentences are figures the card is stating and this is a
     * way out of the card. The gap is the whole of what says so — there is no divider,
     * and adding one would be a third structural device on a card that has one.
     */
    .goal-lines :global(.events-link) {
        margin-top: 0.375rem;
    }
</style>
